{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Root-cause evidence for the 502s: **the MCP process has already been OOM-KILLED on this host today, and it is currently running at 2.4x the RSS it died at.** This is live, not historical.\n\n**The kill, from `dmesg -T`:**\n\n    [Sat Sep  5 03:13:03 2026] git invoked oom-killer: gfp_mask=0x140dca(...), order=0, oom_score_adj=0\n    [Sat Sep  5 03:13:03 2026] oom-kill:constraint=CONSTRAINT_NONE, global_oom,\n        task_memcg=/system.slice/docker-10b90b10...scope, task=rebar-mcp, pid=185499, uid=0\n    [Sat Sep  5 03:13:03 2026] Out of memory: Killed process 185499 (rebar-mcp)\n        total-vm:2683168kB, anon-rss:1893156kB, file-rss:0kB, shmem-rss:0kB\n\nNote the shape: **`git` invoked the OOM killer** — it asked for memory, none was available, and the kernel killed the largest resident process, which was `rebar-mcp` at **1.89 GB**. The MCP was not the requester. It was the victim, chosen for being biggest.\n\n**Current state, 2026-09-05 12:30 UTC:**\n\n    free -m:  total 7813   used 6851   free 183   available 779   Swap: 0\n    docker stats:\n      rebar-mcp-59d504c2bdbb-8093   4.566GiB / 7.631GiB   59.84%\n      compose-gerrit-1                723.9MiB            9.26%\n      compose-review-bot-1            689MiB              8.82%\n      compose-opcert-1                36.91MiB            0.47%\n    ps -eo rss: rebar-mcp 4618256 KB\n\nSo the MCP alone now holds **59.8% of host RAM**, against 1.89 GB when the kernel killed it nine hours ago. There is **no swap**, and 183 MB free.\n\n`mem_used_percent` shows a STEP, not a drift: `58 61 86 85 87 86 88 87` over the last two hours — a jump from ~60% to ~87% roughly two hours ago, held since. The current MCP container (`rebar-mcp-59d504c2bdbb-8093`) has been up 2 hours, which matches.\n\n**Why this is this ticket's mechanism, not a neighbour.** An OOM kill of `rebar-mcp` produces exactly the symptom this ticket is named for: the endpoint stops answering. And this ticket already records that a transient MCP exit became a PERMANENT 502 on 2026-09-02 — because the retire sweep can remove an exited container that nginx still points at. So \"OOM kills MCP\" and \"MCP 502s persist\" compose into an outage, and the trigger is any `git` invocation large enough to exhaust the remaining headroom. Gates and the review-bot invoke `git` constantly.\n\n**Second-order damage, already happening: monitoring is going blind.** The memory pressure is slowing `observability.sh` enough that its later sections starve. Measured over the same 40-minute window, against ~8 expected datapoints: `root_disk_used_percent` 8/8 (published early in the script), `mem_used_percent` 2/8, `journal_used_percent` 2/8, `docker_storage_used_percent` 2/8, `container_stats_ok` 1/8, `replication_errors` 1/8, `data_disk_debris_bytes` **0/8**. Fifteen alarms are currently in ALARM, and the great majority are `treat_missing_data=breaching` dead-men firing on those gaps rather than on any real breach — the mechanism documented on `illbred-sour-alpaca`.\n\nThat includes `container_memory_rss_bytes{service=mcp}`, which returns NO DATA for the last ten hours. **The metric that would have shown this growth is itself a casualty of it.**\n\nI am taking a controlled `docker restart` of the MCP container now, under standing operator authorisation to restart as needed. Reasoning stated plainly: a controlled restart at a quiet moment is strictly better than a second uncontrolled OOM kill at an arbitrary one, and the evidence that a second kill is coming is the first one plus a 2.4x larger target. Result and post-restart verification to follow in the next comment.\n\nWhat this does NOT establish, and someone should: whether 4.57 GB is a leak or a legitimate working set. The store is ~254k commits, so a large in-memory index is not implausible. If it is legitimate, the fix is instance memory or a bounded cache, not a restart — and a restart only buys hours."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788611579303590172,"uuid":"e32f47d3-5d88-489c-a3f5-94453ce286b8"}